    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the total number of games played in the baseball league, we can use the concept of combinations since each team plays three games with each of the other teams exactly once.\n\nFirst, we need to calculate the total number of games if every team played with every other team. Each player would play \\( n - 1 \\) games. In a complete graph with \\( n \\) vertices (playing teams), the number of edges is given by the combination:\n\n\\[ \\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\]\n\nFor our case:\n\n\\[ \\binom{9}{2} = \\frac{9 \\times 8}{2} = 36 \\]\n\nThis means, if each team plays every other team, there would be a total of 36 games.\n\nHowever, our league has only nine teams initially, and they need to play three games each against every other team. Thus, if we divide the complete-game total by nine to account for different sets of games, we must reduce and determine by how much we've over-counted:\n\n\\[ \\text{Additional games per three-fold replacement}=(3\\cdot\\binom{8}{2})=\\left(3\\cdot\\frac{8\\cdot7}{2}\\right)=3\\cdot28=84 \\]\n\nSince there are 3 folds, we divide this total number by three:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total additional games}=\\frac{84}{3}=28 \\]\n\nSo, overall, after considering each team plays three times with another team, we end up with:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total number of games by finishing schedule}=\\left(\\binom{9}{2}-9\\cdot3\\cdot8\\right)\\cdot\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{2} = (36 - 24)\\cdot28 + 28 \\]\n\nWhere 24 = 9 * 3 * 8.\n\nFinally, summing up both calculations:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total number of games}=\\frac{\\binom{9}{2}-9\\cdot3\\cdot8}{2}\\cdot9\\cdot8+\\binom{9}{2}=\\boxed{72} \\]",
